    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures \u2014 the other `*.java` files under `src/test` are the abstract base and a helper, not runnable tests).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` opt-in and its rejection path already existed; the missing piece was the `householdId` \u2014 assigning a stable shared identifier and returning it. I wired that through the full stack:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically). `sharesHousehold` stays `writeOnly` on `OwnerFields`.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **DB schemas** \u2014 added a `household_id` column to the `owners` table in all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres). The tests run under the `hsqldb` profile with `ddl-auto=none`, so the SQL column is what actually matters for them.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `assignHousehold(...)`, called during `addOwner`. When `sharesHousehold` is true and at least one existing owner matches the same last name + address (using the same case-/whitespace-insensitive normalization as the existing duplicate check):\n   - It reuses an existing member's `householdId` if one is present, otherwise derives a stable one from the normalized last name + address (first 12 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256).\n   - It stamps that id onto the new owner **and** back-fills any matching member that lacks it, so the whole household stays consistent.\n   - If the flag is absent, or set but no household exists, no id is assigned.\n\n## How it satisfies the spec\n\nIn the acceptance test, owner `a` is created normally; `b` and `c` then join with `sharesHousehold=true` at `a`'s name/address. `b` triggers generation of the id (also back-filled onto `a`); `c` reuses it \u2014 so `b` and `c` report the same non-blank `householdId`, which is what the test asserts. The pre-existing 409 behavior for duplicates *without* the opt-in is untouched.",
